---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import Steps from "../../components/use-cases/Steps.astro";
import Benefits from "../../components/use-cases/Benefits.astro";
import Note from "../../components/use-cases/Note.astro";
import { Clock, Shield, FileText, Search } from "lucide-react";
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="Private Client Meeting Notes for Independent Consultants | Summit"
  description="Keep client meeting notes organized on your Mac. Use local transcription and summaries, then find past decisions before your next call. Optional cloud AI is your choice."
  audience="Independent Consultants"
  current="consulting"
  heading="Client meeting notes you can find before the next call"
  lead="When you work with several clients, each conversation needs a clear place. Summit turns recorded conversations into transcripts and summaries on your Mac, with folders to organize engagements and search to revisit earlier decisions."
  ctaHeading="Try Summit on your next client call"
  ctaText="Summit runs on macOS. Choose Local Models in AI settings to keep summaries and chat on your Mac."
>
  <h2 class="!mt-0">You choose where client conversations are processed</h2>
  <p>
    Transcription runs on your Mac, and there is no Summit account holding your
    meeting library. Select Local Models and summaries and chat run on your Mac
    too. If you choose a cloud AI provider, it receives transcript excerpts and,
    for chat, your question with related topics, action items and meeting titles
    and dates, so review that setting before recording confidential
    conversations. Markdown auto-export and iCloud sync are off until you turn
    them on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recording consent and your client obligations still apply. The
    <a href="/security/architecture">architecture page</a> lists exactly what is
    stored and what can leave your Mac.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep client context separate in your workflow</h2>
  <Steps
    steps={[
      {
        title: "Create a folder for each engagement",
        body: 'Give every client its own folder, and optionally a folder <a href="/docs/guides/summary-templates">summary template</a> so each meeting is written up the same way.',
      },
      {
        title: "Use clear meeting titles",
        body: "Name meetings with the client and topic, so the right conversation is easy to spot in search results.",
      },
      {
        title: "Check the scope before you ask",
        body: "Filter search by folder, or select that client's meetings and chat about just those. The chat shows which meetings are included before you ask a question.",
      },
      {
        title: "Open the original passage",
        body: "Chat answers cite timestamped transcript passages. Check the source before using an answer in a client deliverable.",
      },
    ]}
  />
  <Note>
    Folders help organize notes; they are not a separate account, encrypted
    vault or access-control boundary.
  </Note>

  <h2>Before the next client call</h2>
  <p>
    Find the earlier meeting, review what was agreed and use its original
    transcript to check dates and commitments. Summaries are a starting point,
    not a guarantee that every detail was captured.
  </p>

  <h2>Why consultants choose Summit</h2>
  <Benefits
    benefits={[
      {
        icon: Clock,
        title: "Start from a draft",
        body: "Summit drafts a summary with key points and action items after each recording, so your write-up starts from notes instead of a blank page.",
      },
      {
        icon: Shield,
        title: "No meeting bot",
        body: "Summit records from your Mac instead of joining the call as a participant, for video calls and in-person conversations alike.",
      },
      {
        icon: FileText,
        title: "Consistent client write-ups",
        body: "Summary templates shape notes for client updates, project documentation and stakeholder follow-ups.",
      },
      {
        icon: Search,
        title: "Recall with sources",
        body: "Search past meetings for specific discussions, decisions or requirements, and follow chat citations back to the passage in the transcript.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Who it's for</h2>
  <h3>Strategy consultants</h3>
  <p>
    Document stakeholder interviews, executive briefings, and strategic planning
    sessions. Keep complex projects on track with searchable meeting archives
    and clear action items.
  </p>
  <h3>Management consultants</h3>
  <p>
    Take notes from discovery calls, process reviews, and client workshops.
    Draft executive summaries and track project milestones.
  </p>
  <h3>Technology consultants</h3>
  <p>
    Record technical requirements gathering, architecture reviews, and client
    demos, then search for the decisions you need to revisit.
  </p>
  <h3>Fractional executives</h3>
  <p>
    Keep a folder for each organization so its context, decisions, and action
    items are easy to find when you switch between engagements.
  </p>
</UseCaseLayout>
